  => STEP 3 PASS: the DUT opened the session and checked the far end's host key.  It printed
     the ED25519 fingerprint "SHA256:0fFTyaQszFtMFRJM74JQfBopyr+KpUyaHdtfx8keFk8" and asked
     whether to continue, which a plain TCP client would never do.  It then authenticated and
     reached the far end's prompt.  "USER", not "PRIV", is the correct success: an AW+ login
     lands at privilege 1, and `cktmp` is a privilege 1 account.  The session is really
     remote, because `show system` through it returns AT-x960-28YSQ, S/N A10737A261700021,
     awplus_main-20261020-78 -- a different model, serial number and build train from the
     DUT.  Both units print the host name "awplus", so the serial number is what settles it.
     The far end records the client's address as `vty 0 cktmp ... 10.38.22.97`, and the
     session closed cleanly back to the DUT's own prompt.

STEP 4 -- host-key trust the DUT cannot show or remove                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The first connection printed:

    Warning: Permanently added '10.38.22.98' (ED25519) to the list of known hosts.

  The second and every later connection asked nothing, so the entry is real and is used.
  It is invisible to configuration management.  The DUT's running-config is byte-identical
  before and after the whole case, so the learned host key is not in the running-config at
  all.  And there is no command to see it or remove it:

awplus# clear ssh ?
      <1-65535>  SSH session ID
      all        All SSH sessions
awplus# show ssh ?
      client  Display SSH client configuration
      server  Display SSH server configuration
awplus# crypto key ?
      generate  Generate public/private key
      install   Install certificate
      remove    Remove certificate
      zeroize   Zeroize public/private key

  `clear ssh` clears SESSIONS, not known hosts.  `show ssh` offers only `client` and
  `server` configuration.  `crypto key` manages the device's OWN keys and certificates, not
  the hosts it has learned to trust.
  => STEP 4 PASS: the client behaves correctly, and this is a product observation worth
     recording.  An SSH client case necessarily leaves state on the DUT that the DUT cannot
     clean up and that no `show` command will reveal.  Three things follow.  A configuration
     difference will never detect it.  A later test that gives the far end a new host key
     will meet a host-key mismatch with no documented way to clear it.  And the teardown of
     any such case is incomplete by exactly this one item, through no fault of the procedure.

